1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,499
This is ECE 358, video 12. We start Chapter 4, the network layer. The topic is the network layer and the architecture of a router.

2
00:00:11,949 --> 00:00:18,242
After this video, you can explain forwarding and routing, and the data plane and the control plane.

3
00:00:18,692 --> 00:00:21,636
You can draw a router and explain its parts.

4
00:00:22,086 --> 00:00:26,353
You can calculate the desirable switching rate, N times R.

5
00:00:26,803 --> 00:00:31,453
You can explain where queues form in a router, and when buffers overflow.

6
00:00:31,903 --> 00:00:36,767
At the end, we solve question bank network questions 1 to 21.

7
00:00:37,217 --> 00:00:48,183
The network layer moves segments from the sending host to the receiving host. Host A is the sender, and host B is the receiver. Each host has all five layers.

8
00:00:48,633 --> 00:00:56,569
Between them are two routers, R1 and R2. A router has only three layers: network, link and physical.

9
00:00:57,019 --> 00:01:02,693
First, the sender. The network layer of host A gets a segment from the transport layer.

10
00:01:03,143 --> 00:01:10,354
It puts the segment in a datagram. A datagram is an IP packet. It adds the network header, H n.

11
00:01:10,804 --> 00:01:14,537
Then host A passes the datagram down to its link layer.

12
00:01:14,987 --> 00:01:23,073
Next, the routers. The network layer is in every Internet device that moves packets: in every host and every router.

13
00:01:23,523 --> 00:01:28,429
Each router reads the header fields of every datagram that passes through it.

14
00:01:28,879 --> 00:01:34,661
It moves the datagram from an input port to an output port, along the end-to-end path.

15
00:01:35,111 --> 00:01:39,804
Last, the receiver. Host B gets the datagram from its link layer.

16
00:01:40,254 --> 00:01:46,569
It takes the segment out of the datagram, and delivers the segment to the transport layer protocol.

17
00:01:47,019 --> 00:01:52,117
The network layer on a router has two key functions. The first is forwarding.

18
00:01:52,567 --> 00:02:02,146
Forwarding moves a packet from the router's input link to the correct output link. Here a packet for destination D arrives on the input link.

19
00:02:02,596 --> 00:02:09,551
The router looks up D in its routing table, R T. The entry for dest. D says output link 2.

20
00:02:10,001 --> 00:02:16,443
So the packet goes out on output link 2. Forwarding is a local action inside one router.

21
00:02:16,893 --> 00:02:23,763
The second function is routing. Routing finds the route that packets take from the source to the destination.

22
00:02:24,213 --> 00:02:30,378
The purple line is one route: from the source through R1, R3 and R4 to D.

23
00:02:30,828 --> 00:02:37,249
Routing algorithms build the routing table. Each entry maps a destination to an output link.

24
00:02:37,699 --> 00:02:44,142
So on a router, given the destination of an IP packet, the table gives the output link.

25
00:02:44,592 --> 00:02:49,115
Two notes from the slide. First, no router stores the full route.

26
00:02:49,565 --> 00:02:53,042
Second, each routing table knows only the next hop.

27
00:02:53,492 --> 00:02:57,033
Here R1 knows only that D is through R3.

28
00:02:57,483 --> 00:03:00,534
R3 knows only that D is through R4.

29
00:03:00,984 --> 00:03:03,907
And R4 knows that D is on its own link.

30
00:03:04,357 --> 00:03:10,074
The network layer has two planes. The data plane is a local function in each router.

31
00:03:10,524 --> 00:03:18,524
A datagram arrives on an input port. Its header has a destination address value, here 0 1 1 1.

32
00:03:18,974 --> 00:03:25,331
The data plane looks up 0 1 1 1 in the local routing table. The entry says port 3.

33
00:03:25,781 --> 00:03:29,707
So the datagram leaves on port 3. This is forwarding.

34
00:03:30,157 --> 00:03:40,247
In short, the data plane is local, per router. It forwards each datagram from an input port to an output port, from its destination address.

35
00:03:40,697 --> 00:03:43,663
The control plane is network-wide logic.

36
00:03:44,113 --> 00:03:52,433
It decides how a datagram is routed among the routers, on the end-to-end path from the source host to the destination host.

37
00:03:52,883 --> 00:03:57,640
In other words, the control plane decides how the routing tables are built.

38
00:03:58,090 --> 00:04:03,743
The two planes work together. The control plane puts a routing table into every router.

39
00:04:04,193 --> 00:04:08,823
The data plane then uses that table to forward each datagram.

40
00:04:09,273 --> 00:04:16,078
And the control plane messages between routers are themselves datagrams, sent by the data plane.

41
00:04:16,528 --> 00:04:21,947
There are two approaches for the control plane. The first is the per-router approach.

42
00:04:22,397 --> 00:04:30,013
Each router has its own routing algorithm component, R A. It is the purple box above the data plane.

43
00:04:30,463 --> 00:04:33,556
Each R A writes the table of its own router.

44
00:04:34,006 --> 00:04:44,822
The routing algorithm components in all routers interact with each other in the control plane. They exchange information, and each one computes its own table.

45
00:04:45,272 --> 00:04:49,283
This is the traditional approach. This course focuses on it.

46
00:04:49,733 --> 00:04:53,679
The second approach is software-defined networking, S D N.

47
00:04:54,129 --> 00:04:58,097
A remote controller runs on servers, outside the routers.

48
00:04:58,547 --> 00:05:02,195
Each router has only a small control agent, C A.

49
00:05:02,645 --> 00:05:10,133
The remote controller computes the forwarding tables. It installs them in the routers, through the control agents.

50
00:05:10,583 --> 00:05:15,789
This is the generic architecture of a router. On the left are the input ports.

51
00:05:16,239 --> 00:05:23,236
On the right are the output ports. These are physical ports, not T C P or U D P ports.

52
00:05:23,686 --> 00:05:30,342
In the middle is the high-speed switching fabric. It connects every input port to every output port.

53
00:05:30,792 --> 00:05:35,869
Above the dashed line is the routing processor. This is the control plane.

54
00:05:36,319 --> 00:05:41,098
The control plane is software. It works in a time frame of milliseconds.

55
00:05:41,548 --> 00:05:44,428
It gives the routing table to each input port.

56
00:05:44,878 --> 00:05:49,337
Below the dashed line is the forwarding data plane. It is hardware.

57
00:05:49,787 --> 00:05:53,947
Now follow one datagram. It arrives at input port 1.

58
00:05:54,397 --> 00:05:58,429
Each port has three layers. L1 is the physical layer.

59
00:05:58,879 --> 00:06:00,713
L2 is the link layer.

60
00:06:01,163 --> 00:06:06,475
L3 is the network layer. In L3, the port looks up the output port.

61
00:06:06,925 --> 00:06:12,451
The switching fabric moves the datagram from input port 1 to output port 3.

62
00:06:12,901 --> 00:06:16,485
Output port 3 sends the datagram on its link.

63
00:06:16,935 --> 00:06:22,609
The data plane does all of this in a time frame of nanoseconds, for every datagram.

64
00:06:23,059 --> 00:06:32,638
Look inside one input port. The first box is line termination. This is the physical layer. It receives the bits, one by one.

65
00:06:33,088 --> 00:06:39,189
The second box is the link layer protocol, in receive mode. An example is Ethernet.

66
00:06:39,639 --> 00:06:42,135
The third box is lookup and forwarding.

67
00:06:42,585 --> 00:06:50,031
The port reads the destination address in the IP header. Then it looks up the output port in the forwarding table.

68
00:06:50,481 --> 00:06:59,121
The forwarding table is in the memory of the input port. Each input port has its own copy. This is decentralized switching.

69
00:06:59,571 --> 00:07:06,867
Each entry is match plus action. The match is the destination address. The action is the output port.

70
00:07:07,317 --> 00:07:15,061
The goal is to complete the input port processing at line speed. Line speed is the rate at which packets arrive.

71
00:07:15,511 --> 00:07:24,044
If datagrams arrive faster than the port can forward them into the switch fabric, they wait. A queue forms in the input port.

72
00:07:24,494 --> 00:07:26,478
This is input port queuing.

73
00:07:26,928 --> 00:07:34,907
Traditional routers use destination-based forwarding. They forward based only on the destination IP address.

74
00:07:35,357 --> 00:07:40,711
The switching fabric moves each packet from its input link to the correct output link.

75
00:07:41,161 --> 00:07:45,065
This router has 5 input ports and 5 output ports.

76
00:07:45,515 --> 00:07:49,014
Each line rate R is 10 gigabits per second.

77
00:07:49,464 --> 00:07:51,491
Here only input 1 sends.

78
00:07:51,941 --> 00:08:01,669
But all 5 inputs can receive packets at the same time, each at 10 gigabits per second. To keep up, the fabric must move all of them.

79
00:08:02,119 --> 00:08:08,732
So the fabric must move 5 parts of 10. 5 times 10 is 50 gigabits per second.

80
00:08:09,182 --> 00:08:16,841
This is the switching rate. It is the metric for a fabric: the rate at which packets move from the inputs to the outputs.

81
00:08:17,291 --> 00:08:26,507
The switching rate is often given as a multiple of the line rate R. For N inputs, the desirable switching rate is N times R.

82
00:08:26,957 --> 00:08:32,802
There are three major types of switching fabric: memory, bus, and interconnection network.

83
00:08:33,252 --> 00:08:42,660
The first is switching via memory. First-generation routers were traditional computers. The CPU controls the switching directly.

84
00:08:43,110 --> 00:08:50,790
A packet arrives at the input port. It is copied over the system bus into the memory. That is bus crossing 1.

85
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:59,965
Then the CPU finds the output port, and the packet is copied from the memory to the output port. That is bus crossing 2.

86
00:09:00,415 --> 00:09:03,978
So each datagram crosses the system bus 2 times.

87
00:09:04,428 --> 00:09:07,351
The speed is limited by the memory bandwidth.

88
00:09:07,801 --> 00:09:13,859
The second type is switching via a bus. The input ports and the output ports share one bus.

89
00:09:14,309 --> 00:09:22,139
A datagram goes from the memory of input port 1, over the bus, directly to the memory of output port 2.

90
00:09:22,589 --> 00:09:24,743
The CPU does not copy it.

91
00:09:25,193 --> 00:09:32,831
But only one datagram can use the bus at a time. Inputs 2 and 3 also have datagrams. They must wait.

92
00:09:33,281 --> 00:09:38,571
This is bus contention: the input ports compete for access to the shared bus.

93
00:09:39,021 --> 00:09:42,435
So the switching speed is limited by the bus bandwidth.

94
00:09:42,885 --> 00:09:50,181
The third type is the modern one: an interconnection network. Examples are the crossbar and the Clos network.

95
00:09:50,631 --> 00:09:56,113
This is a 3 by 3 crossbar. Each input line crosses each output line.

96
00:09:56,563 --> 00:10:01,513
Input 1 sends to output 3, and input 2 sends to output 1.

97
00:10:01,963 --> 00:10:12,885
Each path uses different lines, so both datagrams cross at the same time. This is parallelism. So this fabric performs better than memory or a bus.

98
00:10:13,335 --> 00:10:24,898
But two datagrams to the same output cannot cross at the same time. Input 3 also wants output 3, so it waits. We use this fact on the next slide.

99
00:10:25,348 --> 00:10:31,684
A large switch can be built from stages of smaller switches. This is a multistage switch.

100
00:10:32,134 --> 00:10:42,630
Here an 8 by 8 switch is built from 2 by 2 switches. The first stage has 4 switches with 2 inputs each: 4 times 2 is 8 inputs.

101
00:10:43,080 --> 00:10:49,160
To use the parallelism, the fabric cuts each datagram into fixed-length cells at the entry.

102
00:10:49,610 --> 00:10:55,519
It switches the cells through the fabric, and it puts the datagram together again at the exit.

103
00:10:55,969 --> 00:11:03,969
Now the queues. If the switch fabric is slower than all the input ports together, queues can form at the input ports.

104
00:11:04,419 --> 00:11:15,235
Each packet in the picture shows the number of its output port. Input 3 has a packet for output 1 at the front, and a packet for output 3 behind it.

105
00:11:15,685 --> 00:11:24,069
At time t, input 1 and input 3 both have a packet for output 1 at the front. This is output port contention.

106
00:11:24,519 --> 00:11:29,170
Only one of them can go. Input 1 sends. Input 3 waits.

107
00:11:29,620 --> 00:11:31,839
Input 2 sends to output 2.

108
00:11:32,289 --> 00:11:37,366
Now look at the packet for output 3, behind the front packet in input 3.

109
00:11:37,816 --> 00:11:43,981
Output 3 is idle. But this packet cannot move, because the packet in front of it waits.

110
00:11:44,431 --> 00:11:52,559
This is head-of-the-line blocking, H O L blocking. The datagram at the front of the queue stops the others behind it.

111
00:11:53,009 --> 00:11:57,788
One packet time later, input 3 sends its packet to output 1.

112
00:11:58,238 --> 00:12:09,737
The packet for output 3 is now at the front. It lost one packet time, and output 3 was idle for no reason. It has experienced H O L blocking.

113
00:12:10,187 --> 00:12:17,504
High-performance routers avoid H O L blocking. An input port keeps a separate queue for each output.

114
00:12:17,954 --> 00:12:24,930
Then the packet for output 3 does not wait behind the packet for output 1. It can go at time t.

115
00:12:25,380 --> 00:12:32,292
This is a really important slide. Look at one output port. First, it has a datagram buffer.

116
00:12:32,742 --> 00:12:37,542
Then come the link layer protocol, in send mode, and line termination.

117
00:12:37,992 --> 00:12:42,685
The fabric can deliver datagrams to this port at up to N times R.

118
00:12:43,135 --> 00:12:48,149
But the output link sends at only R. So the output port needs a buffer.

119
00:12:48,599 --> 00:12:58,156
Suppose 3 inputs send to this output at the same time. In each packet time, 3 datagrams arrive, and the link sends only 1.

120
00:12:58,606 --> 00:13:04,195
After one packet time, 3 minus 1, which is 2 datagrams, wait in the buffer.

121
00:13:04,645 --> 00:13:11,941
After two packet times, 2 plus 2 is 4 datagrams. The queue grows by 2 in each packet time.

122
00:13:12,391 --> 00:13:16,637
After three packet times, 4 plus 2 is 6 datagrams.

123
00:13:17,087 --> 00:13:23,636
After four packet times, 6 plus 2 is 8. All 8 slots are used: the buffer is full.

124
00:13:24,086 --> 00:13:34,667
Now there is no free buffer for new datagrams. Arriving datagrams are lost. This is loss from output port buffer overflow, because of congestion.

125
00:13:35,117 --> 00:13:43,288
Two policies control the output buffer. The drop policy decides which datagrams to drop when no buffer is free.

126
00:13:43,738 --> 00:13:48,581
The scheduling discipline chooses which of the queued datagrams to send next.

127
00:13:49,031 --> 00:13:57,116
With priority scheduling, some datagrams get better performance than others. This is related to network neutrality.

128
00:13:57,566 --> 00:14:04,777
Now the question bank. Questions 1 to 3 are short descriptive questions. Pause the video and try them.

129
00:14:05,227 --> 00:14:17,387
Question 1. The network layer moves segments from host to host. The sender puts each segment in a datagram. The receiver takes the segment out and delivers it to the transport layer.

130
00:14:17,837 --> 00:14:21,527
Question 2. Hosts and routers have a network layer.

131
00:14:21,977 --> 00:14:26,799
Links do not. A layer 2 switch has only the link and physical layers.

132
00:14:27,249 --> 00:14:35,953
Question 3. The two functions are forwarding and routing. Forwarding moves a packet from an input link to the correct output link.

133
00:14:36,403 --> 00:14:41,480
Routing finds the route from source to destination, and builds the routing table.

134
00:14:41,930 --> 00:14:47,071
Questions 4 to 7 are about the two planes. Pause the video and try them.

135
00:14:47,521 --> 00:14:56,161
Question 4. The data plane is local, in each router. It forwards each datagram from an input port to an output port.

136
00:14:56,611 --> 00:15:03,395
The control plane is network-wide. It decides the end-to-end route, so it builds the routing tables.

137
00:15:03,845 --> 00:15:12,827
Question 5. Routing messages between routers are IP datagrams. The data plane forwards them like any other datagram.

138
00:15:13,277 --> 00:15:21,127
The slides do not state this directly. It follows because routers exchange routing information over the same links.

139
00:15:21,577 --> 00:15:30,281
Question 6. For each datagram, the data plane looks up the routing table. The control plane builds and installs that table.

140
00:15:30,731 --> 00:15:38,731
Question 7. In the per-router approach, a routing algorithm runs in every router, and the algorithms interact.

141
00:15:39,181 --> 00:15:46,925
In S D N, a remote controller computes the tables and installs them through a control agent in each router.

142
00:15:47,375 --> 00:15:52,709
Questions 8 and 9 are about the parts of a router. Pause the video and try them.

143
00:15:53,159 --> 00:15:58,705
Question 8. A router has input ports, a switching fabric, and output ports.

144
00:15:59,155 --> 00:16:04,339
These parts form the data plane. It is hardware, and it works in nanoseconds.

145
00:16:04,789 --> 00:16:11,829
Above them is the routing processor. It is the control plane. It is software, and it works in milliseconds.

146
00:16:12,279 --> 00:16:20,535
Question 9. An input port has these functions. First, line termination: the physical layer receives the bits.

147
00:16:20,985 --> 00:16:25,700
Second, the link layer protocol in receive mode, for example Ethernet.

148
00:16:26,150 --> 00:16:33,083
Third, lookup and forwarding: the port finds the output port in the forwarding table, at line speed.

149
00:16:33,533 --> 00:16:39,123
It also queues datagrams when they arrive faster than it can forward them into the fabric.

150
00:16:39,573 --> 00:16:45,333
Questions 10 and 11 are about the switching fabrics. Pause the video and try them.

151
00:16:45,783 --> 00:16:55,959
Question 10. The first fabric is memory. The CPU copies each datagram through the memory, so the datagram crosses the bus 2 times.

152
00:16:56,409 --> 00:17:02,937
The second is a bus. All ports share one bus, so they compete for it. This is bus contention.

153
00:17:03,387 --> 00:17:09,040
The third is an interconnection network, such as a crossbar or a multistage switch.

154
00:17:09,490 --> 00:17:19,175
Question 11. An interconnection network performs better because of parallelism. Datagrams to different outputs cross at the same time.

155
00:17:19,625 --> 00:17:24,937
To use this parallelism, the fabric cuts the datagrams into fixed-length cells.

156
00:17:25,387 --> 00:17:30,785
Questions 12 to 14 are about the switching rate. Pause the video and try them.

157
00:17:31,235 --> 00:17:38,936
Question 12. The metric is the switching rate. That is the rate at which packets move from the inputs to the outputs.

158
00:17:39,386 --> 00:17:46,789
Question 13. For N inputs with line rate R, the desirable switching rate is N times R.

159
00:17:47,239 --> 00:17:56,625
Question 14. Each bidirectional link gives one input link and one output link. Link 1 gives input 1 and output 1.

160
00:17:57,075 --> 00:18:01,534
So the router has 5 inputs and 5 outputs. N is 5.

161
00:18:01,984 --> 00:18:06,037
R is 100 gigabits per second, in each direction.

162
00:18:06,487 --> 00:18:09,389
All 5 inputs can receive at the same time.

163
00:18:09,839 --> 00:18:18,393
The desirable switching rate is N times R, which is 5 times 100. That is 500 gigabits per second.

164
00:18:18,843 --> 00:18:25,073
Do not count 10 links. The fabric moves each packet once, from one input to one output.

165
00:18:25,523 --> 00:18:42,035
Question 15. The router has 5 input links and 5 output links at 100 gigabits per second. Explain how each type of buffer can overflow. The question lets us make assumptions. Pause the video and try it.

166
00:18:42,485 --> 00:18:55,839
First, the rates. The desirable rate is N times R: 5 times 100 is 500. The actual rate is 2 times 500, which is 1000 gigabits per second.

167
00:18:56,289 --> 00:19:07,063
Our assumption: the fabric can deliver to one output port at most 2 times R. That is 2 times 100, which is 200 gigabits per second.

168
00:19:07,513 --> 00:19:15,385
Part a. Inputs 1 to 4 all receive traffic for output 5, each at 100 gigabits per second.

169
00:19:15,835 --> 00:19:22,363
So 4 times 100, which is 400 gigabits per second, want output 5.

170
00:19:22,813 --> 00:19:32,306
Only 200 can cross the fabric to output 5. 400 minus 200 leaves 200 gigabits per second at the inputs.

171
00:19:32,756 --> 00:19:35,871
So the input buffers fill and then overflow.

172
00:19:36,321 --> 00:19:44,790
This is output port contention, and H O L blocking makes it worse: datagrams for other outputs wait behind them.

173
00:19:45,240 --> 00:19:53,795
Part b. Now only inputs 1 and 2 send to output 5. 2 times 100 is 200 gigabits per second.

174
00:19:54,245 --> 00:20:01,839
The fabric can deliver all 200 to output 5, because 200 is not more than the limit of 200.

175
00:20:02,289 --> 00:20:11,569
But output link 5 sends only 100. 200 minus 100 leaves 100 gigabits per second in the output buffer.

176
00:20:12,019 --> 00:20:20,467
The output buffer fills and overflows. The cause is that the arrival rate at one output is more than its line rate R.

177
00:20:20,917 --> 00:20:26,955
Questions 16 to 18 are about H O L blocking. Pause the video and try them.

178
00:20:27,405 --> 00:20:33,271
Question 16. H O L blocking occurs at the input port, in the input queue.

179
00:20:33,721 --> 00:20:43,087
Question 17. The datagram at the head of an input queue waits, because its output port is busy with a datagram from another input.

180
00:20:43,537 --> 00:20:48,998
The datagrams behind it cannot move, even when their own output ports are idle.

181
00:20:49,448 --> 00:20:54,504
Question 18. Some output ports stay idle while packets wait for them.

182
00:20:54,954 --> 00:21:02,741
So the throughput goes down, and the queueing delay increases. Input buffers can overflow, so there is more loss.

183
00:21:03,191 --> 00:21:08,908
Questions 19 and 21 are about output buffers. Pause the video and try them.

184
00:21:09,358 --> 00:21:18,275
Question 19. Datagrams can arrive from the fabric at up to N times R, faster than the output link can send them at R.

185
00:21:18,725 --> 00:21:24,741
The buffer holds the datagrams that cannot be sent yet. Without it, they are lost at once.

186
00:21:25,191 --> 00:21:33,917
Question 21. The first policy is the drop policy. It decides which datagrams to drop when no buffer is free.

187
00:21:34,367 --> 00:21:44,479
The second is the scheduling discipline. It chooses which queued datagram to send next, for example by priority. We solve question 20 next.

188
00:21:44,929 --> 00:22:06,475
Question 20. A router has 4 input and 4 output ports at 1 gigabit per second. The switching rate is 4 gigabits per second. Packets are 1500 bytes. Output port 2 has space for 50 packets. Find a scenario that overflows this buffer. Pause the video and try it.

189
00:22:06,925 --> 00:22:13,560
Scenario: inputs 1, 3 and 4 all send a continuous stream of packets to output port 2.

190
00:22:14,010 --> 00:22:25,039
First, one packet time. It is L over R. L is 1500 bytes. 1500 times 8 is 12,000 bits.

191
00:22:25,489 --> 00:22:30,993
R is 1 gigabit per second, which is 10 to the power 9 bits per second.

192
00:22:31,443 --> 00:22:38,931
12,000 divided by 10 to the power 9 is 12 microseconds. This is one packet time.

193
00:22:39,381 --> 00:22:45,184
The 3 inputs send 3 times 1, which is 3 gigabits per second into the fabric.

194
00:22:45,634 --> 00:22:56,386
This is less than the fabric rate of 4 gigabits per second, so the fabric is not the problem. All 3 packets reach output port 2 in each packet time.

195
00:22:56,836 --> 00:23:06,607
But output link 2 sends only 1 packet in each packet time. So the queue grows by 3 minus 1, which is 2 packets per packet time.

196
00:23:07,057 --> 00:23:11,814
The plot shows the queue at output 2. The buffer holds 50 packets.

197
00:23:12,264 --> 00:23:17,277
The orange line is the queue length. It grows by 2 in each packet time.

198
00:23:17,727 --> 00:23:24,938
50 divided by 2 is 25 packet times. At 25 the queue reaches the buffer size.

199
00:23:25,388 --> 00:23:34,476
25 times 12 microseconds is 300 microseconds. After 300 microseconds the buffer is full.

200
00:23:34,926 --> 00:23:43,779
Then, in each packet time, 3 packets arrive but only 1 slot becomes free. So 2 of every 3 arriving packets are lost.

201
00:23:44,229 --> 00:23:51,525
Here is the recap. The network layer is in every host and every router. It puts segments in datagrams.

202
00:23:51,975 --> 00:23:59,719
Forwarding is local: input link to output link. Routing finds the end-to-end route and builds the routing table.

203
00:24:00,169 --> 00:24:07,167
The data plane is per router, in hardware, and fast. The control plane is network-wide, in software.

204
00:24:07,617 --> 00:24:13,611
A router has input ports, a switching fabric, output ports and a routing processor.

205
00:24:14,061 --> 00:24:18,072
The three fabrics are memory, bus, and interconnection network.

206
00:24:18,522 --> 00:24:21,594
The desirable switching rate is N times R.

207
00:24:22,044 --> 00:24:24,775
Input queues cause H O L blocking.

208
00:24:25,225 --> 00:24:32,094
Output queues form when traffic to one output is more than its line rate. They cause delay and loss.

209
00:24:32,544 --> 00:24:38,005
In the next video, we look at the IP datagram format and at fragmentation.
